ez --script host/chez/run-unit.ss\n(import (chezscheme))\n\n(load \"host/chez/run-gate-harness.ss\")\n;; The loader + install source roots, so a row's (require 'stdlib.ns) resolves the\n;; .clj overlay from source exactly like jolt — without this, a require of a ns\n;; whose NATIVE vars exist (e.g. clojure.core.async) silently no-ops via the\n;; ns-has-vars? arm and the overlay fns are missing at analysis.\n(load \"host/chez/loader.ss\")\n;; jolt.ffi's HOST half, in cli.ss's position (after the loader, per its note\n;; there). Without it the gate booted a world holding jolt.ffi's Clojure half and\n;; not its host half, so stdlib/jolt/ffi.clj's fully-qualified reaches for the\n;; primitives (jolt.ffi/__cfn, __remember-size!) resolved to nothing — and the\n;; ffi row, which asserts only :throws, passed on \"Unknown class jolt.ffi\"\n;; instead of on the bad-type error it is written to provoke.\n(load \"host/chez/java/ffi.ss\")\n(set-source-roots! ldr-install-roots)\n;; The base java.time API (stdlib/jolt/time) autoloads on first java.time.* use at\n;; runtime; here we load it once BEFORE the per-case snapshot below so its\n;; value-semantics arms (impl/install-seams!) and class registrations are part of\n;; the stable base world. Otherwise the first java.time case would install those\n;; arms after the snapshot, and zj-reset! — which prunes vars but can't unregister\n;; a Scheme-level arm — would leave them dangling and break later cases. (Cold\n;; autoload is covered per-process by the smoke gate.)\n(load-namespace \"jolt.time.base\")\n\n(load \"host/chez/gate-private-tmpdir.ss\")\n\n(define (slurp path)\n  (call-with-input-file path\n    (lambda (p)\n      (let loop ((cs '()) (c (read-char p)))\n        (if (eof-object? c) (list->string (reverse cs))\n            (loop (cons c cs) (read-char p)))))))\n\n(define cases (jolt-read-string (slurp \"test/chez/unit.edn\")))\n(define kw-suite    (keyword #f \"suite\"))\n(define kw-expr     (keyword #f \"expr\"))\n(define kw-expected (keyword #f \"expected\"))\n(define kw-throws   (keyword #f \"throws\"))\n\n(load \"host/chez/run-case-isolation.ss\")\n\n;; --- run ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(define pass 0)\n(define fails '())              ; (suite expr msg)\n(define suite-pass (make-hashtable string-hash string=?))\n(define suite-total (make-hashtable string-hash string=?))\n(define (bump! ht k) (hashtable-set! ht k (+ 1 (hashtable-ref ht k 0))))\n\n;; A row that never returns used to stall the whole gate at 100% CPU with nothing\n;; printed — in CI, a job that runs to its timeout with no clue which row hung. A\n;; watchdog thread watches the row in flight and, past the limit, names it and\n;; exits nonzero. It never interrupts a row (that could leave the runtime's locks\n;; held mid-update); the process ends instead. JOLT_UNIT_ROW_TIMEOUT overrides the\n;; limit in seconds.\n(define row-limit-secs\n  (or (let ((v (getenv \"JOLT_UNIT_ROW_TIMEOUT\"))) (and v (string->number v))) 120))\n(define row-in-flight #f)      ; (index expr start-seconds) or #f\n(fork-thread\n  (lambda ()\n    (let loop ()\n      (sleep (make-time 'time-duration 0 1))\n      (let ((r row-in-flight))\n        (when (and r (> (- (time-second (current-time)) (caddr r)) row-limit-secs))\n          (let ((p (current-error-port)))\n            (fprintf p \"\\nunit gate: row ~a did not finish within ~as — hung?\\n  ~a\\n\"\n                     (car r) row-limit-secs (cadr r))\n            (flush-output-port p))\n          ;; exit from this thread would only unwind this thread; rt.ss's\n          ;; jolt-exit-process ends the process from any thread\n          (jolt-exit-process 2)))\n      (loop))))\n\n;; What a row raised, rendered: \"raised\" alone left a failure that happened only\n;; under load with nothing to go on.\n(define (raised-msg e)\n  (string-append \"raised \"\n    (guard (_ (#t \"(unprintable)\"))\n      (jolt-repl-str (jolt-unwrap-throw e)))))\n\n(let loop ((i 0))\n  (when (< i (pvec-count cases))\n    (let* ((row (pvec-nth-d cases i jolt-nil))\n           (suite (jolt-get row kw-suite))\n           (expr (jolt-get row kw-expr))\n           (expected (jolt-get row kw-expected))\n           (throws? (eq? expected kw-throws))\n           (sink (open-output-string)))\n      (bump! suite-total suite)\n      (set! row-in-flight (list i expr (time-second (current-time))))\n      (guard (e (#t (if throws?\n                        (begin (set! pass (+ pass 1)) (bump! suite-pass suite))\n                        (set! fails (cons (list suite expr (raised-msg e)) fails)))))\n**C4 — Final track verdict under astra’s criteria**\n\nYes: the standing ruling is **PARTIAL-GO for bounded P1b work, conditional on qualification of each implemented capability; blocking and foreign-thread rows retain honest refusal; P2/P3 remain Chez-only.**\n\nAstra §5 states verbatim:\n\n> **Partial GO:** memory, loading, ordinary calls, and errno may land independently when qualified. Retain blocking/callback refusals and describe the result as partial FFI. This does **not** authorize P2/P3 parity claims.\n\nIts NO-GO criteria are:\n\n> **NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked; unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required; exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown; or the solution depends on silently moving generic calls to another native thread.\n\nThe blocking failure independently sustains NO-GO on both tested configurations. No universal foreign-entry impossibility proof is required to reach that ruling.\n\nThe next-path language remains:\n\n> If 4.9.8 cannot meet the necessary runtime transitions, the next decision is an explicit Gambit upgrade or runtime patch through the upgrade gate. The fallback remains honest refusal, with Chez retaining the affected capability.\n\nP1b must not assert:\n\n- GC-safe blocking or blocking-plus-errno qualification.\n- Generic foreign-pthread callbacks, including serialized entry.\n- Arbitrary dynamic closure-backed callables; F6’s architecture/trampoline constraint remains.\n- Unrestricted yielding, migration, exception escape, or nested callback behavior.\n- Qualified memory, loader, ABI, or public jolt behavior solely from these standalone fixtures.\n- Linux qualification, full FFI parity, or qualification of AOT/FASL/subprocess capabilities.\n\nQualified callable modes can be included only with their own public-path behavioral gates and explicit restrictions.\n\n**C5 — Readiness for a P1b brief**\n\n**No further runtime experiment is necessary before writing the bounded brief.** It should:\n\n1. Pin the exact runtime/configuration; MT is an unsuccessful experiment, not a qualified replacement.\n2. Carry the inventory Q1–Q6, F6 trampoline constraint, real-GC replacement, and per-capability refusals.\n3. Define callable ownership and lifetime rules, catch exceptions inside callbacks, and reject unsupported native-thread entry before executing Scheme.\n4. Require target-identified public jolt tests, both platform legs for acceptance, negative controls, patch/seed reproducibility, rollback, and preserved regressions (`Plan:267–275`).\n5. Correct or explicitly exclude the MT teardown and timing claims above.\n\nLinux and remaining lifecycle experiments are prerequisites for future qualification claims, not blockers to documenting and implementing the bounded slice.\n\nCONFIRMED-VERDICT — PARTIAL-GO for bounded P1b memory/loader/ordinary-call/errno work and independently qualified callable modes; blocking and foreign-thread rows remain refused, with P2/P3 Chez-only unless a separately chosen upgrade/runtime-patch path passes qualification.\nhost/gambit/host-ffi.ss:156:               (sa-foreign-bytes-set! (+ (jnum->exact ptr) (jnum->exact off)) bv (bytevector-length bv))\nhost/gambit/host-ffi.ss:158:                 (sa-foreign-bytes-set! (+ (jnum->exact ptr) (jnum->exact off) (bytevector-length bv)) z 1))\nhost/gambit/host-ffi.ss:202: