{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '575,597p;601,632p;721,736p;845,875p' host/chez/java/natives-array.ss; rg -n 'array-cells|make-lazy-cseq' host/chez/seq.ss | head -46; rg -n 'delay|class.*byte|jolt-array-kind' host/chez/{java/class-model.ss,protocols.ss,java/records-interop.ss,printing.ss}; tail -10 build/p1c-r2/remint.log; tail -6 build/p1c-r2/yin-baseline.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '575,597p;601,632p;721,736p;845,875p' host/chez/java/natives-array.ss; rg -n 'array-cells|make-lazy-cseq' host/chez/seq.ss | head -46; rg -n 'delay|class.*byte|jolt-array-kind' host/chez/{java/class-model.ss,protocols.ss,java/records-interop.ss,printing.ss}; tail -10 build/p1c-r2/remint.log; tail -6 build/p1c-r2/yin-baseline.log\"","aggregated_output":";; The seq form. Routed through na-list->backing rather than a per-kind store loop\n;; so the prefix is packed by exactly the rule the (T-array coll) constructor packs\n;; by — byte narrowing, the flvector/fxvector choice, and the boxing fallback for\n;; an element the backing cannot hold are all decided in one place.\n;;\n;; A nil init seqs to nothing, which lands on an all-default array: that is the\n;; JVM's answer too (RT.seq(null) is null, so its fill loop runs zero times).\n(define (na-init-prefix-backing n kind init default)\n  (let* ((n (let ((n (exact n))) (na-check-length n) n))\n         (taken (let loop ((i 0) (l (seq->list (jolt-seq init))) (acc '()))\n                  (if (or (fx=? i n) (null? l))\n                      (reverse acc)\n                      (loop (fx+ i 1) (cdr l) (cons (car l) acc))))))\n    (na-list->backing (append taken (make-list (- n (length taken)) default)) kind)))\n\n;; (T-array size) | (T-array size init) | (T-array seq)\n(define (na-num-array a rest init kind)\n  (if (number? a)\n      (if (and (pair? rest) (not (na-scalar-init? kind (car rest))))\n          (make-jolt-array (na-init-prefix-backing (na-idx a) kind (car rest) init) kind)\n          (make-jolt-array (na-make-backing (na-idx a) kind (if (pair? rest) (car rest) init)) kind))\n      (na-from-seq a kind)))\n\n;; A byte-array element is a SIGNED byte, -128..127, like the JVM's byte[]: a value\n;; above 127 reads negative, so (neg? b), a (bit-and b 0xff) mask, and arithmetic\n;; over a high byte all behave as they do on the JVM. na-byte-of is the ONE place a\n;; value entering a byte array is narrowed — Byte.byteValue() semantics: truncate\n;; toward zero (the JVM's d2i, not floor — (byte-array [-1.9]) is -1, not -2), take\n;; the low 8 bits, fold the sign bit. na-bytearray->bv masks back to 0..255 at the\n;; raw-byte seam, so the two carriers round-trip byte-exactly.\n(define (na-u8->byte b) (if (fx<? b 128) b (fx- b 256)))\n;; The fixnum arm is the same answer by a shorter route, not a second rule: a\n;; fixnum IS its own (exact (truncate v)), so all that is left of the general\n;; case is the mask and the fold. Worth splitting because the general case spends\n;; three GENERIC (bignum-capable) operations to discover that — 14.5ns — and every\n;; door into a byte array comes through here: into-array, Arrays/fill,\n;; na-list->backing, and the untyped aset.\n(define (na-byte-of v)\n  (if (fixnum? v)\n      (na-u8->byte (fxand v #xff))\n      (let ((b (na-integral-of v 8)))\n        (if (fixnum? b) b (jolt-num-cast-throw v)))))\n\n;; What a value becomes when it is STORED in a primitive array: Number's\n;; xxxValue, which is how Numbers.T_array fills one from a seq or an init and how\n;; the JVM narrows a primitive. An int[] holds (int) of what it is given — 1.7 is\n;; 1, 1/2 is 0, 1.5M is 1 — a double saturates at the int range first, and a\n;; short[] or byte[] keeps the low bits of that; a long[] wraps a bignum to 64\n;; bits; a double[] holds the double value and a float[] the nearest float.\n;; A value the JVM would refuse (a string into an int[], its ClassCastException)\n;; is stored as it is, as before: the superset, and the backing widens to hold\n;; it. A byte[] alone refuses, as its bytevector backing has nowhere to put it.\n(define (na-coerced-kind? kind) (memq kind '(int long short byte double float)))\n(define (na-elem-of kind v)\n  (case kind\n;; (byte-array n [init]) | (byte-array coll). Also coerces the host's OTHER byte\n;; carrier — a Chez bytevector (what the charset encoders produce) — and a string's\n;; UTF-8 bytes, so bytevector and byte-array interconvert across interop seams.\n(define (na-byte-array a . rest)\n  (cond\n    ;; na-num-array, not na-make-backing directly: byte-array keeps its own cond\n    ;; for the bytevector/string doors below, but the size+init door is the same\n    ;; one every other constructor uses and needs the same seq form — before\n    ;; jolt-6g1 (byte-array 3 [1 2]) died in na-byte-of trying to narrow the vector\n    ;; itself to a byte, where the JVM answers [1 2 0]. An out-of-range scalar\n    ;; still narrows exactly as before (na-make-backing's byte arm).\n    ((number? a) (na-num-array a rest 0 'byte))\n    ((bytevector? a) (na-bv->bytearray a))\n    ((string? a) (na-bv->bytearray (string->utf8 a)))\n    (else (na-from-seq a 'byte))))\n;; jolt byte-array -> Chez bytevector (for String decode / utf8->string), the\n;; made (first big-array) or (take 4 bytes) allocate ~100 bytes per element of\n;; the array (#1548). Same shape as str->seq (seq.ss); the tail is one more\n;; ArraySeq of the same flavor. A cell's head is the array itself, under an\n;; sk-array-cells kind: cseq-first (seq.ss) reads the array on every first, at the\n;; length less the cell's count, so a later aset is seen by any cell at that index,\n;; as the JVM's is.\n(define (arr->seq a i)\n  (let ((n (ja-len a)))\n    (if (fx>=? i n) jolt-nil\n        (make-cseqn a (make-lazy-src lz-array-seq a i) (na-cells-kind a) jolt-nil (fx- n i)))))\n(define (na-cells-kind a) (fx+ (na-seq-kind a) (fx- sk-array-cells-seq sk-array-seq)))\n(define lz-array-seq\n  (register-lazy-src! 'array-seq (lambda (a i) (arr->seq a (fx+ i 1)))))\n(define (array-cells-index a cnt) (fx- (ja-len a) cnt))\n;; reduce over an array seq from the cell with CNT left: the elements straight off\n;; the array, reduced honored and unwrapped, as reduce-seq's own loop does\n(define (array-seq-reduce f acc a cnt)\n  (let ((direct? (procedure? f)))\n    (let rec ((acc acc) (i (array-cells-index a cnt)))\n      (cond ((jolt-reduced? acc) (jolt-reduced-val acc))\n            ((fx>=? i (ja-len a)) acc)\n            (else (let ((x (ja-ref a i)))\n                    (rec (if direct? (jolt-site-kept (f acc x)) (jolt-invoke2 f acc x)) (fx+ i 1))))))))\n;; first's read: in range by construction (a cell's count is 1..length), so the\n;; backing is read once and indexed without ja-ref's bounds check\n(define (array-cells-first a cnt)\n  (let* ((v (jolt-array-vec a)) (x (ja-backing-ref v (fx- (ja-backing-len v) cnt))))\n    (if (flonum? x) (na-fl-elem a x) x)))\n(set-array-cells-ops! array-cells-first array-seq-reduce)\n(register-seq-arm! jolt-array? (lambda (c) (arr->seq c 0)))\n(define %na-nth jolt-nth)\n87:;; sk-array-cells-* below, which are what an array's own seq is made of now.\n100:(define sk-array-cells-seq    25)\n101:(define sk-array-cells-int    26)\n102:(define sk-array-cells-long   27)\n103:(define sk-array-cells-short  28)\n104:(define sk-array-cells-double 29)\n105:(define sk-array-cells-float  30)\n106:(define sk-array-cells-bool   31)\n107:(define sk-array-cells-byte   32)\n108:(define sk-array-cells-char   33)\n374:;; sk-array-cells kinds above, and its head is the ARRAY, not an element: first\n382:;; to array-cells-reduce, a loop over the array with no cells, as ArraySeq.reduce\n384:(define array-cells-read (lambda (arr cnt) (error 'seq-first \"no array seqs on this host\")))\n385:(define array-cells-reduce (lambda (f acc arr cnt) (error 'reduce \"no array seqs on this host\")))\n386:(define (set-array-cells-ops! read reduce) (set! array-cells-read read) (set! array-cells-reduce reduce))\n387:(unless (and (= sk-array-cells-seq 25) (= sk-array-cells-char 33))\n388:  (error 'seq.ss \"array-cells-kind?'s kind range is stale\"))\n389:(define-syntax array-cells-kind?\n394:             (if (array-cells-kind? (cseq-kind s))\n395:                 (array-cells-read (cseq-head s) (cseqn-cnt s))\n901:         (or (fx=? k sk-string-seq) (and (fx>=? k sk-array-cells-seq) (fx<=? k sk-array-cells-char))))\n2048:        ((array-cells-kind? (cseq-kind s))\n2049:         (array-cells-reduce f acc (cseq-head s) (cseqn-cnt s)))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:558:        ((and (jolt-array? obj) (eq? (jolt-array-kind obj) 'byte)) '(\"[B\" \"Object\"))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:559:        ((and (jolt-array? obj) (eq? (jolt-array-kind obj) 'char)) '(\"[C\" \"Object\"))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:560:        ((and (jolt-array? obj) (eq? (jolt-array-kind obj) 'int)) '(\"[I\" \"Object\"))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:561:        ((and (jolt-array? obj) (eq? (jolt-array-kind obj) 'long)) '(\"[J\" \"Object\"))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:562:        ((and (jolt-array? obj) (eq? (jolt-array-kind obj) 'double)) '(\"[D\" \"Object\"))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:565:         (let ((k (jolt-array-kind obj)))\nhost/chez/protocols.ss:623:        ;; delay, a future, a promise, a reduced box, a chunk buffer, a ref —\nhost/chez/java/records-interop.ss:170:;;   - a reference or deferred value (atom, var, ref, volatile, delay, promise,\nhost/chez/java/records-interop.ss:187:;; 20-45x a record's check (#1299). future / promise / agent / delay live in\nhost/chez/java/records-interop.ss:363:     (or (jolt-promise? val) (jolt-future? val) (jolt-delay? val) (jolt-lazyseq? val)))\nre-minted seed (converged after 2 pass(es))\ngen-seed: backend target -> :gambit\ngen-seed: prelude #3% occurrences: 0 (all inside string data, none as op uses)\ngen-seed: image #3% occurrences: 1 (all inside string data, none as op uses)\ngen-seed: prelude grep-verified (no #3% / (foreign-procedure op uses)\ngen-seed: image  grep-verified (no #3% / (foreign-procedure op uses)\ngen-seed: wrote host/gambit/seed/prelude.ss (2397728 bytes)\ngen-seed: wrote host/gambit/seed/image.ss (2038412 bytes)\nmint: 0 form(s) skipped\ngen-seed: gambit seed minted on Chez\ngambitffi: local toolchain provenance verified (7 inputs)\nsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 60 cc -O1 -dynamicli